### Why do duplicates happen in Kafka?

Duplicates happen because of the network gap between the broker writing a record and the producer receiving the acknowledgement. The producer sends a record. The broker appends it to the log. But the acknowledgement is lost on the way back. The producer cannot tell whether the write succeeded, so it retries. Without protection, the same record is now in the log twice.

```java
package com.bank.duplicate;

public class DuplicateScenario {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Step 1: Producer sends transfer for SAV2001");
        System.out.println("Step 2: Broker writes to partition 3 at offset 100");
        System.out.println("Step 3: ACK lost on the network");
        System.out.println("Step 4: Producer retries the same record");
        System.out.println("Step 5: Broker writes AGAIN at offset 101");
        System.out.println("Result: Priya debited TWICE for the same transfer!");
    }
}
```

```text
Step 1: Producer sends transfer for SAV2001
Step 2: Broker writes to partition 3 at offset 100
Step 3: ACK lost on the network
Step 4: Producer retries the same record
Step 5: Broker writes AGAIN at offset 101
Result: Priya debited TWICE for the same transfer!
```

### How does the idempotent producer fix this?

When idempotence is enabled, the broker gives the producer a unique producer id (PID). The producer attaches a sequence number to each record per partition, increasing by one each time. The broker remembers the last sequence it accepted for that PID and partition. If a retried record carries a sequence that equals or is less than the last written, the broker recognizes it as a duplicate, acknowledges it, but does not append it again.

```java
package com.bank.producer;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerConfig;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;
import java.util.Properties;

public class IdempotentFixDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "broker1:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        // Enable idempotence (default in recent Kafka)
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ENABLE_IDEMPOTENCE_CONFIG, "true");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ACKS_CONFIG, "all");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.RETRIES_CONFIG, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        props.put(ProducerConfig.MAX_IN_FLIGHT_REQUESTS_PER_CONNECTION, 5);

        try (KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props)) {
            producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("transfers", "SAV2001",
                "{\"amount\":50000,\"type\":\"DEBIT\"}"));
            // If ACK is lost and producer retries, broker drops the duplicate
            System.out.println("Idempotent: retry-safe, no double debit for Priya");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Idempotent: retry-safe, no double debit for Priya
```

### What are the configuration requirements?

Idempotence requires three settings to work:

| Setting | Required value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| enable.idempotence | true | Turns on PID + sequence tracking |
| acks | all | Record must be on all ISR before ack |
| max.in.flight.requests.per.connection | <= 5 | Broker can detect and reorder within 5 |
| retries | > 0 | Retrying is the whole point |

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class IdempotenceRequirements {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Idempotence requirements:");
        System.out.println("  enable.idempotence = true (default in Kafka 3.0+)");
        System.out.println("  acks = all");
        System.out.println("  max.in.flight.requests.per.connection <= 5");
        System.out.println("  retries > 0");
    }
}
```

```text
Idempotence requirements:
  enable.idempotence = true (default in Kafka 3.0+)
  acks = all
  max.in.flight.requests.per.connection <= 5
  retries > 0
```

### How does idempotence connect to exactly-once?

Exactly-once has three parts. First, the write must not duplicate on retry -- the idempotent producer. Second, grouped writes must be atomic -- the transactional producer. Third, in a consume-process-produce loop, offsets and output must be committed together with consumers reading only committed data.

```java
package com.bank.eos;

public class ExactlyOnceLayers {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Exactly-once = three layers:");
        System.out.println("  Layer 1: Idempotent producer (no retry duplicates)");
        System.out.println("  Layer 2: Transactional producer (atomic multi-write)");
        System.out.println("  Layer 3: read_committed consumer (see only committed)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Idempotence is the BASE. Without it, exactly-once fails.");
    }
}
```

```text
Exactly-once = three layers:
  Layer 1: Idempotent producer (no retry duplicates)
  Layer 2: Transactional producer (atomic multi-write)
  Layer 3: read_committed consumer (see only committed)

Idempotence is the BASE. Without it, exactly-once fails.
```

### What are the limitations of idempotence?

Idempotence is scoped to a single producer session and per partition. Across a producer restart the producer id changes, so idempotence alone does not dedup across sessions. It also does not protect against the application choosing to resend different data, nor against duplicate side effects in external systems.

```java
package com.bank.limitation;

public class IdempotenceLimitations {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Idempotence does NOT protect against:");
        System.out.println("  1. Application-level resends (different PID after restart)");
        System.out.println("  2. External system side effects (database insert)");
        System.out.println("  3. Cross-partition duplicates (scope is per-partition)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("For these, you need:");
        System.out.println("  - Transactions (stable transactional.id survives restarts)");
        System.out.println("  - Idempotent consumers keyed on business id");
    }
}
```

```text
Idempotence does NOT protect against:
  1. Application-level resends (different PID after restart)
  2. External system side effects (database insert)
  3. Cross-partition duplicates (scope is per-partition)

For these, you need:
  - Transactions (stable transactional.id survives restarts)
  - Idempotent consumers keyed on business id
```

### Why does this matter in banking?

A duplicated fee, settlement, or transfer is money charged twice or created out of nothing. This is a correctness and compliance failure, not a performance blip. Removing retry duplicates is a hard requirement.

```java
package com.bank.impact;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class BankingImpact {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal transferAmount = new BigDecimal("50000.00");

        System.out.println("Without idempotence:");
        System.out.println("  Priya's balance debited: " +
            transferAmount.multiply(new BigDecimal("2")) + " INR (DOUBLE!)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("With idempotence:");
        System.out.println("  Priya's balance debited: " +
            transferAmount + " INR (correct)");
        System.out.println("  Retry duplicate silently dropped by broker");
    }
}
```

```text
Without idempotence:
  Priya's balance debited: 100000.00 INR (DOUBLE!)

With idempotence:
  Priya's balance debited: 50000.00 INR (correct)
  Retry duplicate silently dropped by broker
```

### What are common mistakes with idempotent producers?

The most common mistake is assuming idempotence protects against application-level duplicate sends, such as a retry in your business logic that creates a new producer record with a new sequence number. Another error is setting `max.in.flight.requests.per.connection` above 5, which silently disables idempotence in older Kafka versions. A third mistake is relying on idempotence across producer restarts, since the producer id changes on restart and the broker treats it as a new producer.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class IdempotenceMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: assume app-level resend is deduped");
        System.out.println("  producer.send(record); // seq=5, PID=7");
        System.out.println("  // App retries with new record");
        System.out.println("  producer.send(newRecord); // seq=6, PID=7 -- NOT a dup!");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("WRONG: max.in.flight > 5");
        System.out.println("  max.in.flight.requests.per.connection=10");
        System.out.println("  // Idempotence silently disabled in Kafka < 3.0");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: enable.idempotence=true, acks=all, in-flight<=5");
        System.out.println("  And use transactions for cross-restart guarantees");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: assume app-level resend is deduped
  producer.send(record); // seq=5, PID=7
  // App retries with new record
  producer.send(newRecord); // seq=6, PID=7 -- NOT a dup!

WRONG: max.in.flight > 5
  max.in.flight.requests.per.connection=10
  // Idempotence silently disabled in Kafka < 3.0

CORRECT: enable.idempotence=true, acks=all, in-flight<=5
  And use transactions for cross-restart guarantees
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Idempotence is the non-negotiable base layer. Transactions use a stable transactional id to fence zombies and preserve guarantees across restarts. True end-to-end exactly-once also requires making downstream consumers idempotent on a business id, because Kafka cannot roll back a row already inserted in PostgreSQL. The cost of idempotence is negligible; the cost of transactions is real latency and coordinator overhead.
